/* Domain 3.0 — Security Architecture (18% of SY0-701). Original questions. */

QB("3.1", "Cloud & Infrastructure Models", [
  ["In the cloud shared responsibility model for IaaS, who is responsible for patching the guest operating system?",
    ["The customer", "The cloud provider", "The ISP", "No one, it's automatic"], 0,
    "In IaaS, the provider secures the physical infrastructure and hypervisor. The customer manages the OS, applications, data, and identities. In SaaS, the provider handles almost everything except data and user access."],
  ["A company uses Microsoft 365 for email. Which cloud service model is this?",
    ["SaaS", "IaaS", "PaaS", "On-premises"], 0,
    "Software as a Service delivers a complete application managed by the provider. Customers manage their users and data."],
  ["Developers deploy code to a cloud platform that manages the OS, runtime, and scaling for them. Which model is this?",
    ["PaaS", "IaaS", "SaaS", "Hybrid"], 0,
    "Platform as a Service provides a managed platform (runtime, middleware, OS) so developers just deploy code."],
  ["An organization runs sensitive workloads on-premises and uses a public cloud for burst capacity. This is a:",
    ["Hybrid cloud", "Community cloud", "Private cloud only", "Air-gapped network"], 0,
    "Hybrid cloud combines private/on-prem and public cloud. It creates security considerations around consistent policies, identity, and connectivity between environments."],
  ["Which architecture lets developers run code as functions without managing servers, paying only when the code runs?",
    ["Serverless", "Monolithic", "Bare metal", "Mainframe"], 0,
    "Serverless (function as a service) means the provider manages all infrastructure. The attack surface shifts to the function code, permissions, and event triggers."],
  ["Breaking an application into small, independent services that communicate through APIs is called:",
    ["Microservices architecture", "Monolithic architecture", "Air-gapping", "Centralized mainframe"], 0,
    "Microservices are scalable and resilient (one service failing doesn't kill everything), but they add many APIs to secure."],
  ["Defining servers, networks, and firewall rules in code templates so environments can be deployed consistently is:",
    ["Infrastructure as Code (IaC)", "Shadow IT", "Software-defined radio", "Air gapping"], 0,
    "IaC (like Terraform or CloudFormation) makes deployments repeatable and auditable, and lets security scan configurations before deployment."],
  ["Which networking approach separates the control plane from the data plane so the network can be programmed centrally?",
    ["Software-defined networking (SDN)", "Air-gapped network design", "Token ring topology", "Physical network isolation"], 0,
    "SDN centralizes network control in software. That makes it agile, but the controller becomes a high-value target that must be secured."],
  ["A critical control system has NO physical or network connection to any other network. This is called:",
    ["Air-gapped", "Segmented with VLANs", "Microsegmented", "Hybrid"], 0,
    "An air gap physically isolates a system. It's strong but not perfect: removable media can still bridge the gap (Stuxnet is the classic example)."],
  ["Industrial systems that control a power plant's turbines and valves are known as:",
    ["ICS/SCADA", "SaaS", "Serverless", "VDI"], 0,
    "Industrial control systems (ICS) and SCADA monitor and control physical processes. They often run legacy systems that are hard to patch, so segmentation is critical."],
  ["Which is a common security concern with IoT devices?",
    ["Weak default credentials and infrequent firmware updates", "They always require hardware MFA tokens to operate", "They can't connect to standard IP-based networks", "They use encryption so strong it slows the network"], 0,
    "IoT devices often ship with default passwords, limited security features, and rare updates. Put them on their own segmented network."],
  ["A medical device with limited processing power and a fixed purpose runs a real-time operating system (RTOS). What's a key security challenge?",
    ["It may not support patching or standard security tools", "It always includes a full graphical user interface", "It runs too many general-purpose apps at once", "It can never be connected to any network at all"], 0,
    "Embedded systems and RTOS devices have constrained resources and long lifecycles, which makes patches and endpoint agents hard to apply. Segmentation and monitoring compensate."],
  ["When choosing an architecture, which consideration addresses how quickly a system can be restored after failure?",
    ["Ease of recovery", "Power consumption", "Compute cost only", "Color of the hardware"], 0,
    "Architecture considerations include availability, resilience, cost, responsiveness, scalability, ease of deployment, ease of recovery, patch availability, inability to patch, power, and compute."],
  ["Centralized architecture is often criticized because:",
    ["It can create a single point of failure", "It's impossible to manage", "It's always cheaper", "It removes the need for backups"], 0,
    "Centralized systems are easier to manage but can be a single point of failure. Decentralized designs spread risk but are harder to manage consistently."],
]);

QB("3.2", "Secure Infrastructure & Appliances", [
  ["A web server that must be reachable from the internet should be placed in a:",
    ["Screened subnet (DMZ)", "Internal LAN with domain controllers", "Air-gapped network", "Guest Wi-Fi VLAN"], 0,
    "A screened subnet (formerly DMZ) holds public-facing services, separated by firewalls from both the internet and the internal network, so a compromise there doesn't directly expose internal systems."],
  ["Administrators must first connect to one hardened server before they can reach servers in a secure zone. That server is a:",
    ["Jump server", "Proxy server", "Load balancer", "Honeypot"], 0,
    "A jump server (jump box) is a hardened, monitored access point into a secure zone. It controls and logs administrative access."],
  ["Which device sits between users and the internet, caching content and filtering requests on their behalf?",
    ["Forward proxy", "Load balancer", "Jump server", "WAF placed in front of internal APIs"], 0,
    "A forward proxy makes requests for internal users, enabling caching, URL filtering, and inspection. A reverse proxy sits in front of servers for inbound requests."],
  ["Which device distributes incoming traffic across multiple web servers to improve availability?",
    ["Load balancer", "IDS", "Jump server", "HSM"], 0,
    "Load balancers distribute traffic, remove failed servers from rotation, and can offload TLS. Active/active uses all servers at once, and active/passive keeps standbys ready."],
  ["A network sensor receives a COPY of traffic from a switch port mirror and alerts on suspicious activity, but can't block it. This is a:",
    ["Passive monitoring device (IDS)", "Inline IPS", "Fail-closed firewall", "Jump server"], 0,
    "Passive devices (on a TAP or port mirror) see copies of traffic and can only alert. Inline (active) devices like an IPS sit in the traffic path and can block."],
  ["If a security appliance fails, it stops all traffic from passing to preserve security. This is configured as:",
    ["Fail-closed", "Fail-open", "Active/active", "Port security"], 0,
    "Fail-closed (fail-secure) blocks traffic on failure, prioritizing security. Fail-open lets traffic pass on failure, prioritizing availability."],
  ["Which firewall type can make decisions based on the specific application (like blocking Facebook games but allowing Facebook) regardless of port?",
    ["Next-generation firewall (NGFW)", "Stateless packet filter", "Layer 2 switch", "Load balancer"], 0,
    "NGFWs perform application-aware (Layer 7) inspection, often with IPS, URL filtering, and identity integration."],
  ["Which device protects web applications by inspecting HTTP traffic for attacks like SQL injection and XSS?",
    ["Web application firewall (WAF)", "Stateless packet filter firewall", "VPN concentrator appliance", "NAT gateway with port forwarding"], 0,
    "A WAF understands HTTP/HTTPS and blocks web attacks at the application layer. It's often required by PCI DSS for public web apps."],
  ["Which standard provides port-based network access control, requiring authentication before a switch port allows traffic?",
    ["IEEE 802.1X", "802.11ac", "802.3af", "SNMPv1"], 0,
    "802.1X uses a supplicant (client), authenticator (switch or AP), and authentication server (usually RADIUS). It's used for wired and enterprise wireless access control."],
  ["Which protocol is used with 802.1X to carry authentication methods like certificates and passwords?",
    ["EAP", "SMTP", "SNMP", "FTP"], 0,
    "The Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) is a framework that carries various authentication methods (EAP-TLS, PEAP, EAP-TTLS) between the supplicant and the authentication server."],
  ["Which type of VPN connects two entire offices so all traffic between them is encrypted automatically?",
    ["Site-to-site VPN", "Client-to-site VPN", "Clientless SSL portal", "Split DNS"], 0,
    "Site-to-site VPNs connect networks, usually with IPsec between gateways. Client-to-site (remote access) VPNs connect individual users."],
  ["An IPsec mode that encrypts the ENTIRE original packet, including the original IP header, is:",
    ["Tunnel mode", "Transport mode", "AH-only mode", "Passive mode"], 0,
    "Tunnel mode wraps the whole original packet in a new one, which is common for site-to-site VPNs. Transport mode encrypts only the payload, keeping the original IP header."],
  ["Which IPsec protocol provides integrity and authentication but NOT encryption?",
    ["Authentication Header (AH)", "Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP)", "TLS", "SSH"], 0,
    "AH provides data integrity and origin authentication. ESP provides encryption (confidentiality), and can also provide integrity."],
  ["Which technology combines networking and security (SWG, CASB, ZTNA, firewall) into a cloud-delivered service for remote users?",
    ["SASE", "SD-WAN only", "VLAN trunking", "NAT"], 0,
    "Secure Access Service Edge (SASE) delivers security services from the cloud, close to users wherever they are. SD-WAN optimizes WAN connectivity and is often paired with SASE."],
  ["Which switch feature limits the number of MAC addresses on a port and can shut down the port if an unknown device connects?",
    ["Port security", "Spanning tree", "Port mirroring", "Trunking"], 0,
    "Port security restricts which, or how many, MAC addresses can use a switch port, which helps stop unauthorized devices from connecting."],
]);

QB("3.3", "Data Types, States & Classification", [
  ["Data stored on a laptop's hard drive is in which state?",
    ["Data at rest", "Data in transit", "Data in use", "Data in motion"], 0,
    "Data at rest is stored (disks, databases, backups). Protect it with full-disk or database encryption and access controls."],
  ["Data being processed in a computer's RAM and CPU is called:",
    ["Data in use", "Data at rest", "Data in transit", "Archived data"], 0,
    "Data in use is actively being processed, often decrypted in memory. Protections include secure enclaves and memory encryption."],
  ["Customer data crossing the internet between a browser and a web server is:",
    ["Data in transit", "Data at rest", "Data in use", "Data sovereignty"], 0,
    "Data in transit (in motion) moves across networks. TLS, IPsec, and VPNs protect it."],
  ["Laws requiring that data about a country's citizens be stored and processed within that country relate to:",
    ["Data sovereignty", "Data masking", "Data in use", "Tokenization"], 0,
    "Data sovereignty means data is subject to the laws of the country where it's located. Many regulations restrict cross-border data transfers. Geolocation of data matters."],
  ["Which data classification would MOST likely apply to unreleased product designs and source code?",
    ["Confidential/Proprietary (trade secret)", "Public (freely releasable)", "Unclassified (no restrictions)", "Open data (shared by policy)"], 0,
    "Trade secrets and intellectual property are typically labeled confidential or proprietary and get strong protection. Classification drives how data is handled."],
  ["Medical records about a patient are BEST described as which data type?",
    ["Protected health information (PHI)", "Public directory information", "Payment card data (PCI)", "Anonymized statistical data"], 0,
    "PHI is health information tied to an individual. It's regulated (for example, by HIPAA in the U.S.) and requires strict privacy and security controls."],
  ["Which classification typically requires the LEAST protection?",
    ["Public", "Restricted", "Sensitive", "Critical"], 0,
    "Public data can be freely disclosed without harm. Sensitive, restricted, and critical data need progressively stronger protection."],
  ["Which method restricts access to data based on the user's or device's physical location?",
    ["Geographic restrictions (geofencing)", "Hashing with a salt", "Data tokenization", "Load balancing by region"], 0,
    "Geographic restrictions use location (IP geolocation, GPS) to permit or deny access, which helps enforce data sovereignty and block risky regions."],
  ["Which technique BEST protects a database backup stored with a third-party cloud provider?",
    ["Encrypting it before upload and keeping the keys under company control", "Compressing it with ZIP so the files are smaller and harder to read", "Renaming the backup files so attackers won't recognize their contents", "Storing it in a public bucket so it's easy to restore during outages"], 0,
    "Encryption at rest with customer-managed keys keeps data confidential even if the provider's storage is breached."],
  ["Splitting sensitive data across many systems so no single system holds a complete, readable record is related to:",
    ["Segmentation of data", "Data masking", "Steganography", "Data sovereignty"], 0,
    "Segmenting data across locations limits what any single breach exposes. It's one of several data protection methods along with encryption, hashing, masking, and tokenization."],
  ["A company labels documents 'Internal Use Only' so employees know how to handle them. What does this support?",
    ["Data classification", "Data sovereignty", "Load balancing", "Key escrow"], 0,
    "Classification labels tell people and tools (like DLP) how to handle data: who can access it, whether it can leave the company, and how it must be stored."],
]);

QB("3.4", "Resilience & Recovery", [
  ["A company keeps a fully equipped facility with live, replicated data that can take over within minutes. This is a:",
    ["Hot site", "Warm site", "Cold site", "Mobile site"], 0,
    "A hot site is ready to go with hardware, software, and current data, so it's the fastest recovery and the most expensive. A warm site has some equipment and older data. A cold site is just space and power."],
  ["Which recovery site provides only space, power, and cooling, with no equipment?",
    ["Cold site", "Hot site", "Warm site", "Cloud site"], 0,
    "A cold site is cheapest but slowest: you must bring in equipment and restore data before you can operate."],
  ["Which RAID level mirrors data across two drives?",
    ["RAID 1", "RAID 0", "RAID 5", "RAID 10 without mirroring"], 0,
    "RAID 1 mirrors data so either drive can fail. RAID 0 stripes with no redundancy. RAID 5 stripes with parity (survives one drive failure). RAID 10 is a stripe of mirrors."],
  ["A backup that copies all data changed since the LAST FULL backup (and doesn't reset the archive bit) is a(n):",
    ["Differential backup", "Incremental backup", "Full backup", "Snapshot"], 0,
    "Differential backups grow each day until the next full backup. Restoring needs the full plus the latest differential. Incrementals back up changes since the last backup of any type, so a restore needs the full plus every incremental."],
  ["Which backup type requires the full backup plus EVERY backup since then to restore?",
    ["Incremental", "Differential", "Full", "Mirror"], 0,
    "Incremental backups are fast and small, but restoring needs the full backup plus every incremental in order."],
  ["Which device provides short-term battery power so systems keep running during brief outages and can shut down gracefully?",
    ["UPS", "Generator", "PDU only", "Surge protector"], 0,
    "An uninterruptible power supply (UPS) bridges short outages and covers the gap until a generator starts. Generators provide long-term power."],
  ["A team walks through a disaster scenario in a conference room, discussing each person's response without touching systems. This is a:",
    ["Tabletop exercise", "Failover test", "Full simulation", "Penetration test"], 0,
    "Tabletop exercises are discussion-based, low cost, and good for finding gaps in plans and communication. Failover tests and simulations actually exercise systems."],
  ["Using servers from multiple hardware vendors and software from different developers so one flaw doesn't take down everything is:",
    ["Platform diversity", "Capacity planning", "Load balancing", "Data masking"], 0,
    "Diversity (platforms, vendors, cryptography, cloud providers) avoids a single common weakness causing a total failure."],
  ["Planning to make sure enough people, technology, and infrastructure exist to meet demand during growth or an emergency is:",
    ["Capacity planning", "Geographic dispersion", "Journaling", "Tokenization"], 0,
    "Capacity planning addresses people (staffing), technology (servers, licenses), and infrastructure (power, bandwidth) so the organization can meet demand."],
  ["Placing data centers in different regions so a hurricane can't take out all of them is called:",
    ["Geographic dispersion", "Clustering in one rack", "Air gapping", "Data masking"], 0,
    "Geographic dispersion spreads sites so a single regional disaster doesn't take down everything."],
  ["Multiple servers work together and appear as one system, so if one fails another takes over. This is:",
    ["Clustering", "Snapshotting", "Journaling", "Tokenization"], 0,
    "Clustering provides high availability: the nodes share the workload or fail over to each other."],
  ["Which backup approach records every change to a database in a log so the system can be restored to a specific point in time?",
    ["Journaling", "Cold site", "RAID 0", "Air gapping"], 0,
    "Journaling records transactions or changes before they're committed, allowing point-in-time recovery and protecting against corruption from crashes."],
  ["Why should backups be stored offsite or offline (immutable)?",
    ["So ransomware or a site disaster can't destroy both production data and backups", "So restores always run faster than restoring from local disks on site", "So the backups don't need to be encrypted while stored at the other site", "So RAID arrays at the primary site can be safely turned off afterward"], 0,
    "Offsite and offline/immutable backups survive physical disasters and ransomware that tries to encrypt or delete reachable backups. Also test restores regularly."],
]);
